12. Faithful Servants

The causes of religious belief are so obvious, that they must perpetually operate on mankind; and though the particular forms of superstition may change, the general principles must remain nearly the same in all ages.
(David Hume, The Natural History of Religion) 

The essence of religion is the projection of human consciousness into an external divine being.
(Ludwig Feuerbach, The Essence of Christianity)

Religion is a powerful force. It is ubiquitous to every society and plays a pivotal role in the lives of most humans. Yet it is elusive. When I say religion is elusive, I mean its underpinnings are elusive. Not its effects. We are very familiar with religion as a cultural phenomenon, and the many explanations, rewards, and social functions it provides. But I take it religion is more than a cultural phenomenon. It is unbounded.
	Before jumping in, I should tidy up my general theory. To start, I take it the Godhead, or the primitive idea of God, was already there long before there was religion; manifesting in the mind at least since the beginning of consciousness. But God, in my opinion, was never quite able to exert his full power through the archetypes. One reason being that God resides in the Conscious and the Archetypes reside in the Unconscious. Another, that God, being an idea, is non-physical; and the Archetypes, being memories, are physical. Though they do interact. I have been freely using the terms Conscious and Unconscious throughout. So I should at this point clear up what they mean. I take the Conscious to include all aspects of consciousness in the mind along with the primitive ideas. And I take the Unconscious to include all unconscious content in the mind along with the archetypes. Importantly, both the Conscious and Unconscious have their own causal powers. With this in mind, we might surmise a first genesis. 
	I speculate the original objects of worship were archetypes, in the sense that I've been arguing. This was pre-linguistic. Though it would be unwarranted to call mere archetype worship religion, at least not the early stages of it. But something like religiousness does form, in my view, when simple words (names) get attached to archetypes. I talked about this process earlier with my discussion of words.  I take name-archetype worship to be the likely progenitor to animism and some of the other primitive hunter-gather religious models.  Names have power. Names allow us to talk about people/places/things that aren't present. They allow us to fabricate people/places/things that don't exist. And they allow us to project. Largely, it provides a means to publicly (and privately) personify the natural world: giving it a soul. In this sense, I believe, names are what provided the original soul-stuff which elevated natural reverence in the first believers to the status of faith. 
	But, and maybe to some criticism, this still doesn't constitute a proper religion. Religion requires sophisticated language and a rich oral tradition to be viable.  Importantly, an oral tradition allows for a mythology. I take mythology to be a defining feature of religion. Without a mythology, religions are susceptible to dissolution and corruption over time. I looked at several myths in the previous sections. And what stands out is that myths attempt to offer an explanation. They offer a justification for why the world is the way that it is or why certain things are the way they are. In this sense, religions that make use of myths possess a modicum of rationality. And I take it this added rational aspect greatly contributes to a religion's survivability and success. Although, we might also point to its practical benefits in this regard.
	As mythologies became more complex it was possible for animism and the other primitive hunter-gather religions to evolve into polytheism, in the typical sense. Though I am ignoring the details of what was likely a complicated and long process. There are also additional factors that contributed to this shift, like the development of agriculture and transitioning to urban centers. But I still hold polytheism would not have been possible without an already existing complex mythology. Though which caused which can be debated. After this important shift, I take it, is when God Himself, rather than nature, is really allowed to shine. The reason is this. If instead of embracing the millions of spirits of nature we limit ourselves to a dozen or even a few hundred gods, we can say a lot about them. We can give them spectacular personalities and backstories and place them in crucial roles throughout our mythology. They are interesting and cause us to think about them frequently. (Which of course is God's goal. To occupy our consciousness) The animist hunter-gatherer may have revered the soul of the deer he killed, but likely wasn't completely enthralled by it. Interesting gods make for long-lasting and powerful gods.
	Enthrallment is paramount, and is God's means of propagation and sustainment. There are various ways to do this. Mythology is one means, aided by a rational principle. Another means is the manipulation of emotion. Surprisingly, God became a master at this, even though He, being an idea, is emotionless. Language is the key. The right language can create mental images in the mind capable of evoking emotions. Odin, for instance, is not just some bland celestial. He is the god of wisdom, war, sorcery, and the dead. He is one-eyed—his other sacrificed to Mimir for knowledge. He has a never-missing spear named Gungnir and a pair of ravens named Huginn and Muninn that scout the world and bring news to him. He invented the Runes and can see into the future. This mental image we now hold in our mind is intoxicating. We admire Odin, and respect him, and like him, and fear him—in direct response to the verbal description. Even something as simple as this, we must concede, overshines any of the archetypes. But nature rarely gives up anything useful. God has an additional trick. Words and language can be used to combine archetypes with ideas. We can also make Odin the Father, and even the Wise Old Man. We just need to use the right words to hijack these archetypes. This creates an emotional powerhouse capable of overriding our outdated circuitry.  
	If only this was enough. Unfortunately, God is a maximizer. In the course of religion's development, God will also make use of artifacts like statues, monuments, talismans, shrines, home altars, prayers, holy books, and sacraments to enthrall us. Where each time we encounter such religious artifacts the idea of God is likely to be triggered in the mind. He will also conscript priests and prophets to proselytize—and push mythologies, tenets, and rituals on us.  (Where listening to selected stories/and tenets and participating in rituals can reinforce the idea of God.) In some cases, He will reorganize entire societies to make worship and the pious life its primary focus. He will also use Lies and make threats against non-believers (i.e., damnation after death, karma, bad luck, getting struck by lightning). With all this in play, God can be on the minds of His faithful servants almost all the time. The standard of success!
	With that said, I want to take a moment to talk about the nature of God. Feuerbach says that God is simply a projection of human consciousness. In some sense this is true. God is tied to consciousness. But I should make some important distinctions. While religion is man-made, God is not. God, while attached to consciousness, is an independent entity. I would not go as far as to say God is a person. But God is an Agent, with preferences, intentions, goals; and things can go better or worse for Him. Though to be clear, whatever prudence or cunning God has is parasitic on the psychological profile of whatever human subject He inhabits. This can make God quite complex, but we should be aware that it is only a by-product of a process pursuing the much simpler directive of maximal sustainment and propagation.
	It is also important to be clear that God is the real benefactee of religion. Not humans. Any benefit humans receive from religion is indirect. And humanity could have achieved that same benefit even if God was simply a mere projection as Feuerbach claims. So we do not need God. But He needs us. This leads us to ask what God Himself actually provides or adds to religion? It's not exactly clear. God is like the Stone in the Stone Soup Fable.  He is the cause for why many religions are beautiful and filled with such wonderful cultural ingredients. But we do all the work and add all the ingredients. If God ate the delicious "soup" it would be fitting. But it's far worse. God instead eats our consciousness, and the soup (religion) is the way He makes it into our brains. I'll go into more detail about the Godhead later.  But at this point I just want to signal to the religious shopper: Buyer Beware!   
	Let us return to religion itself. Hume claims that all religions have the same structure and function: mainly, to provide psychological comfort, remove fear of the unknown, and alleviate anxiety about the future. While I take this to be true, Hume does acknowledge that there are some important differences between types of religions, particularly between polytheism and monotheism. He additionally holds that monotheism is the more developed of the two.  (Hume also acknowledges primitive religions like animism and totemism, but includes them under the umbrella of polytheism.) The most essential difference between polytheism and monotheism is that, in polytheism the gods are not the creators or cause of nature, merely its stewards. They also make mistakes in carrying out their duties and come into conflict with one another. This usually isn't the case in monotheism. In (developed) monotheisms, God is in some way above nature or its cause. This can change the character of how God is revered and worshipped. 
	I take the above to be the defining difference between polytheism and monotheism. Though there are a number of other notable differences.  But before talking about monotheism, it is worth pointing to what monotheism is not, or that which I take to be a primitive monotheism. Historically, it was not uncommon for a society to adopt a main god or give one god more importance over others (such as a father or king of a pantheon). It would also sometimes be the case that some older insignificant God from a pantheon or from nature would increase his status over time and be adopted as the local god of some ethnic group—eventually becoming the sole god of that group.  Some god may also be made generic enough—an every-god—to have more appeal to diverse groups of people.  A god may also be made the primary god or sole god for only a short period of time before returning to a pantheon or becoming insignificant.  I take none of these cases to be monotheism, in its substantial form. I hold this to be the case, even if one of the gods in question absorb all of the powers of the other gods as well as control over all their domains. In my opinion, this is just polytheism cleaned up and with less gods (in this case one).  While there may be less gods, there isn't a significant leap capable of capturing monotheism as we would typically understand it. Instead, I take it, monotheism, in the substantial sense, requires us to create God by means of a rational process. God must be completely disjointed from polytheism and reimagined rather than evolve from it.  Below is one method for deriving a monotheistic god independent of a polytheistic origin. (I am also ignoring the historical genesis of the three current major monotheistic religions; mainly, Judaism, Christianity, and Islam.) 
	In my view, for monotheism to develop, a non-derivative artificiality must be introduced into religion, changing God's perceived nature. To maintain a substantial monotheistic god is a huge undertaken. It is a rational project, not simply a matter of faith. To construct a monotheistic god, we first must tackle the fact that God alone is ultimately the creator or cause of all of nature. This is much harder to account for than responsibility over some small domain (as was the case with polytheism). To adequately explain God's dominion, we are then forced to account for Nature itself. In doing so, we discover that there is an inherent orderliness in nature.  This forces us to also conceive God as orderly, or rational, rather than capricious—if He is the creator or cause of nature. (Of course, we could challenge this assumption. Though it would be odd to say that an irrational god could/or would create an orderly nature.) But by making this adjustment, we impose an intellectual standard on God. We place God within a logical configuration with nature or at least place limits on His ability or willingness to stop the flow of nature's orderliness.  
	The next adjustment has to do with God's Oneness. To maintain Oneness, we must abandon the concept of God as an infinite entity with many forms (as is the case with polytheism).  But this requires us to explain why God in fact would/could not appear in many or every form. The only fix, capable of preserving God's greatness, is to make God simply Infinite—eventually, perfect. But in doing so, God becomes more abstract and essentially paralyzed, unable to act. Largely, why would a singular perfect entity ever want to change¬? But such a god would not be desirable for human beings. So, we must make a final intellectual adjustment, that firmly asserts that God is good and benevolent, and maximally so. Otherwise, we wind up with mere pantheism or deism , which I do not take to be a substantial monotheism. The rational project coming to completion. 
	The above model represents a substantial monotheism and is adequately rational. This rational component, in principle, gives it greater appeal and survivability. But its success also makes it vulnerable in certain ways (e.g., compared to polytheism). One vulnerability is that a mythology is not needed for it to work. It is self-sustaining. But believers might find such a religion less fulfilling without the emotional and psychological benefits of a mythology. (Though, I should point out, all of our current monotheistic religions do possess mythologies. But these mythologies don't play a pivotal role in these religions and are in many ways superfluous.) Another vulnerability with the model is that God appears undeveloped and abstract. While in some ways this is a virtue, lowering the possibility that God will contradict Himself—or call into question His power or goodness—it makes Him less interesting and captivating.
	A third vulnerability is that the rational and intellectualist foundation of a substantial monotheism requires greater justification and understanding for religious beliefs. I.e., In what sense is God dependent on nature, or independent of nature? Why is God not Non-One? Or the classic: Why does God, who is benevolent (and powerful) allow evil in the world? This can lead to frustration and doubt, increasing crises in faith—more so than in polytheism or non-substantial versions of monotheism. The question then to ask—for believers—is whether fideistic vulnerability is a price worth paying in order to maintain an intellectualized theism? 	Finally, I must point out that the model I have illustrated says nothing about the true nature of God Himself. Rather, it simply gives a rational structure of monotheistic religion in general centered around the worship of one sole god, rather than multiple gods. The model can also be differentiated from worship of the beings/objects of nature—as is the case in animism and some of the other primitive hunter-gatherer religions. I also acknowledge that monotheism, in itself, need not be rational and can for the most part be based on pretty much anything as a simple matter of faith.  But such religions would lack justification, appeal, and survivability as was the case with primitive monotheistic religions that have come and gone. 
	That's my religious exposition. But it wasn't all for naught. I hold pushing monotheism to its limit will ultimately lead to philosophy. That is, the next monistic project after removing mythology and embodiment from religion would be to remove the deity; and attempt to explain the Rational Principle that orders nature itself. That is where philosophy takes off, and will be where the next section begins. Interestingly though, in my view, philosophy sprouts from an attempt to perfect religion. And I take this to be the reason philosophy still carries much religious baggage, as well as the unconscious desire to actually complete the goal in question. 


13. Rational Animals

Philosophy is to have conversation with the divine. 
(Plato, Republic)

There is something divine in each of us. 
(Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics) 

Philosophy is Born. The birth of philosophy brought a new system. A system where lone sages, to be called philosophers, would challenge tradition and authority; and decide for themselves what was reasonable and possible. It was a way of thinking that was both critical and bold—where nothing was safe from examination. The philosophical tradition really starts with Thales, who claimed that the world was ultimately made of water.  Though ridiculous it may sound, it was a revolution in how we analyze reality. Unfortunately, much of what Thales said was lost in time, including a lot of rich philosophical activity to spur during the century or two that followed.  So, we must rely on a later point of historical inception coming from Socrates and the tradition that followed him, where the most direct line passes to Plato and Aristotle. The Revolution was in essence, a shunning of social convention and cherished preconceptions; a questioning of the poets and historians and experts and culture keepers; A sharp turn away from mythology, gods, dogma, religion, epics, folkways, emotion; and I would also say archetypes; toward a system based solely on rationality and ideas. It was, in effect, an awakening of Reason. 
	But what exactly is Reason? This is a lot harder to answer than it seems. I said Reason was the primary instrument of ideas for interacting with the world and the human organism. It is also commonly taken to be the source of will and volition, as well as a means to make fine decisions. For Plato and Aristotle, Reason is treated as divine. Though I would say Reason is only divine in the sense that it deals with ideas, which are in some ways themselves divine in nature, or at least exotic in their non-physicality. But I would like to simplify Reason, at least in the practical sense. I take Reason to be all of your ideas focused into a single point of consciousness. We might take this to encompass your Idea, or your own peculiar configuration of rationality. Although, to be clear, this is not the same as your Self or personality. 
	Your Idea, guided by Reason, at least if you listen to most philosophers, is the best or higher part of your nature. But there is also a lower (though I would say different) non-rational nature which involves impulses, desires, emotions, and the human body as a whole—that is your animal essence. (I take the neater demarcation to be between the Mind and the human organism. But this will be argued for later.) There is much debate over how the different parts should relate to one another. Plato takes the appropriate relation to be similar to a rider and his horse. Your idea, or rational part, seeks to tame the non-rational part to do what it commands. Though this might be too generous to Plato. A rider usually cares at least some about his horse. But if you read Plato's Letters it's very clear that his conception of the relationship is meant to be parasitic and hostile. The body is a mere vessel, at best a slave to be used and subdued. 
	Aristotle offers a much kinder model. For Aristotle, the rational part is more like a steward or mentor. It is not there to rule or tame the non-rational part of the human organism but rather to guide it toward what is best. The emotions, for instance, are able to listen to the rational part.  In this sense, the relationship is mutualistic, considering the rational part also seeks what is best. Though I think there is something deeper than mutualism that is achievable. I believe it is also possible to possess the Good contained within one's rationality or Idea. While ideas, and Reason, lack emotion; in my view; the Good has an aesthetic feature that appreciates and values the non-rational part, even some semblance of weak feeling that can care for it.  This is a fluke of nature, and not all people will possess the Good within their Idea or rational faculty. 
	One important application of Reason, as an excellence, is phronesis, or practical wisdom. This usually is taken to involve fine decision-making as well as emotional soundness. This dual requirement can be summed up in the famous line in the Nicomachean Ethics: "Virtue makes the goal right, practical wisdom the things leading to it" (1144a7-8). But this line, famous as it may be, might not be very important. I'm always surprised by how very few virtues Aristotle lists, usually only around ten or fifteen. The truth is there are likely hundreds of virtues. In embracing virtues there need not be some crown of wisdom to decorate. Most of what's needed to get along are useful rules of thumb. The doctrine of the mean, for instance, need to be so profound, much less the definition of virtue itself.  It is a good general rule of thumb that we should avoid getting caught up in emotional extremes. But this need not just be advice for emotions. Pursuing any extreme in most matters of life tends to be a bad idea. Where virtue really shines is in its beauty. It's a spectacle to witness. But then again, maybe no more so than watching a great athlete or great sculptor at work; unless we want to elevate virtue in status just for the mere sake of doing so. I don't want to get caught up in normativity or a discussion of the authority of morality. I only have a single ethical rule in my system,  so there is no reason for me to enter the moral debate. Nor do I want to defame the Great Aristotle in any way or his noble virtue project. Virtue is a great thing, and admirable, and good, and likely necessary to be happy in most cases. But I will add one caveat. Virtue can get you killed. A single act of dishonor to save your own life, or avoid some great misfortune, may not be the worst thing in the world, if you can forgive yourself.
	A major insight of the Aristotelian virtue project is the realization that emotions are something that can be worked with. They are not merely things that must be suppressed or overcome as necessary. As was said, emotions can/do listen to Reason. But it is important to point out that the real work, as Aristotle acknowledges in many places, is being done by Habit. Emotions listen to Reason. But they listen to habit even better. Habit, to be clear, is a type of memory, and therefore (in my theory) has the power to influence behavior and affect emotions. But in habitual action there doesn't seem to be very much planning or reflection or examining of mental images, for that matter, involved. There doesn't seem to be much of anything besides the pure action itself. It is automatic and smooth. The explanation here is that memories and mental images are still involved, and guiding behavior and emotions; it just happens to be unconscious memories and unconscious mental images doing the job. 
	But while habit does most of the grunt work, Reason is still king, or council if we want to stick to the softer description. It does need to occasionally step in and override habit, in order to respond to unique or pressing cases, that may require a little more flexibility and adaptiveness. For instance, if you're having trouble rousing the spirit to care about your neighbor's wretched condition due to unforeseen bad luck, you may need to imagine that fate on yourself in the mind, or even push the scenario to an imagined extreme like your neighbor and his whole family dying due to inactivity; to increase your anger and bring it up to par in order to motivate you to act. Reason tends to get it right, but it is entirely emotionless. To aid it you must provide the emotions yourself; even if this involves fabricating false mental images. Luckily, Reason contains a creative aspect and can generally provide the requisite mental images to bring about its goals if you try. Though, this involves the interaction of Reason, ideas, and memories whose mechanism is somewhat unexplained by my view. But I take it we can all do this, and interaction can/does occur.
	Next, there is the temptation to overgeneralize practical wisdom to encompass every type of good decision-making. But this stretching out of phronesis, at least in the correct use of the term, may be using it improperly. In most cases of good decision-making you can just look to your typical expert or body of knowledge for guidance. There is no need for a moral expert. You don't need a virtuous agent, or phronesis, to tell you how to plan a vacation, or make a bunch of money, or fix your roof. A travel agent, or financial advisor, or roofer will do just fine. There is instead a specific context of practical wisdom which I take to involve virtues and happiness. Though these two things are unsurmountable. And this may tell you that you don't need the vacation, or extorting people is a bad way to make money, or fixing your roof is less urgent than attending your kid's soccer match. And in an important sense this is what philosophy is about. 
	Finally, we might ask what the end of human Reason should be? Most simply, in special regard to phronesis, it is to bring about one's own happiness with the help of virtue. But there is also a higher goal for human beings, in particular philosophers: knowledge or contemplation. This is the topic of the next section. 


14. Nous

All men naturally desire knowledge.
(Aristotle, Metaphysics)

Aristotle opens his Metaphysics by pointing to the importance of knowledge. When doing philosophy this does seem to be most of all what we're caught up with or after. But it is not just any knowledge that philosophers care about, but rather deep knowledge or understanding. This can be referred to as nous. And this is what's really behind the call of philosophy. Its standard of propagation (of ideas) is clarity. Clarity is an aspect of understanding; understanding being something I'll go into. Mainly, an idea you understand is more likely to be remembered and consciously reflected on. An idea you understand is also much easier to explain and communicate to others. Understanding tends to beget more understanding, so to speak. If I had to be more precise, clarity in addition also makes people feel good or happier. In fact, people who have experienced great clarity might even describe it as a higher grade of happiness. I don't want to make any commitments here regarding happiness. Wisdom and contemplation would still be good in themselves and likely pursued even if no good feeling or happiness was present. So, for our purposes let's leave happiness in the domain of phronesis, or practical wisdom. In setting up the argument, we should start with wisdom as it is typically defined or used.     
	Usually when talking about knowledge and wisdom we seem to take it to involve predictive power or the ability to draw useful generalizations. The proclamation goes that a man becomes wise through experience. He has learned that dark clouds will lead to rain, and plans accordingly. But I have something different in mind when referring to wisdom. It might not require experience at all. In fact, it is possible to be wise, yet a practical fool. For instance, the very wise Thales, our first philosopher with the name, is fabled to have died because he fell into a well due to gazing at the stars rather than paying attention to where he was going. Any reasonable person would know that reckless walking leads to injury. The slave that mocked Thales' comical end after witnessing it, was just as capable if not more so than Thales at drawing generalizations based on experience, even sound causal explanations and associations, such as reckless walking leads to injury on account of vision being impaired and out of sync with the body; Or even broad generalizations such as all reckless behavior leads to injury. But, while useful, this is not what wisdom is in the proper or more elevated sense. Wisdom, or theoretical wisdom, I take it, instead is excellence at finding relations between ideas. Though this is more difficult than it seems or to do correctly. Something above ideas is required, which we typically call understanding. But before moving to understanding some quick words are in order about my chosen standard.
	Knowing the "mere" relations of ideas, is sometimes belittled by philosophers. Some of the greatest philosophers in history being earnest hecklers. The very usual example is Bachelors are Unmarried Men. Big whoop they say. I beg to differ. This statement tells me exactly what a bachelor is. I can truly say I understand bachelorness, and have no room for doubt. But it's uninteresting the Great Philosopher goes on. Again, I would have to disagree. I can imagine a world where almost every man is married. In such a world a bachelor would be unusual and interesting. I may eagerly wonder why he is unmarried or chooses to be unmarried. If I search further, I may then wonder what a man is, or a human for that matter. I may then wonder what it means to be married, and why there should be some connection between being a man and being married—or not being married. It will then require me to conclude that some other person is necessary for marriage to occur. I.e., you can't marry yourself. When I take it even further and find the meaning of 'maleness' and 'marriedness', as well as their underlying programs and subroutines involved, I actually wind up with something epistemically satisfying. So, I would contend even very simple relations of ideas are worthy of contemplation, and even more so important relations and those that build the nexi of and allow for the understanding of many other relations and relations of relations.
	Understanding I admit is hard to understand. What I will say is that Reason makes understanding possible, and that it will involve a type of intentional conscious inner volition that searches. Somehow it can find real relations between ideas or establish which ideas or further relations of ideas serve as the foundation(s) for others. When this is done well and relations are found or certain ideas are established as more necessary or foundational than others, we have nous or nous ideas. This encompasses wisdom and could be taken to be a special or particularly valuable kind of knowledge. But, interestingly, I should point out, neither justification nor truth nor belief need be required for nous. This of course rejects the typical definition of knowledge as justified true belief. Though I admit justification, and truth, and belief may be required for knowledge simpliciter. But I don’t take it nous, as something above or better than knowledge, needs to strictly fit these parameters. It gets a pass. For example, I’ve spent a considerable portion of my life thinking about Aristotle’s hylomorphism.  And I do take myself to understand it quite well, as much as a person, or philosopher, can. But I definitely don’t believe it, nor think it’s true; and there is no substantial justification for it at all. But if given the choice, there is really nothing one could trade me in exchange for this flawed abstraction. In all seriousness I would turn down the full range of all knowledge in history or economics or geology in order to preserve Aristotle’s most peculiar falsehood in my mind. Mainly because it is nous, and nous has immeasurable or absolute value. Though maybe not all nous needs to be this grand or beyond negotiation. 
	But to be clear, nous isn’t faith or valuable simply because you cherish it. It must allow you to establish relations between other important ideas or be foundational to those ideas. So if there is some standard we might apply it would be necessity. Necessity is something that is independent of our beliefs. In the mind at least, certain ideas are either necessary for or relate to other ideas or they do not. You cannot force necessity. And this is the primary tool of the theoretical philosopher in engaging the very esoteric and bewildering task of contemplation. Fortuitously for these nous-chasers the further and more abstract one goes into the mind the more necessary ideas become. And in this sense more foundational ideas have greater epistemic-potency. Though some unifying concept(s) or understanding that explains how many surface level ideas work together is valuable as well. But there is a historical bias in contemplative philosophy. The more foundational ideas have more value and are more likely to be nous. First principles have and will likely always be more highly prized, at least as long as philosophers rooted in the Western tradition are still around. 
	Now, as I claimed prior, there are ideas within other ideas, or programs within greater programs. The search for the source codes, or the most foundational ideas or idea is the final end of contemplation: the ultimate Nous. And when thinking of philosophy or philosophers this is what we take to be their unique or most important activity, as far as the field goes, or at least the best use of Reason we can think of. But don’t be so quick to bestow accolades. The necessity we have been talking about only applies to the Mind. And the foundation(s) of the mind could be entirely arbitrary. The source codes might be something illusory like Time, or even something silly like God. These things might not correspond to reality at all. There may be no Time or no God in the world outside of the mind. But let’s hope there is at least Time in the external world. Otherwise, the entire basis on which all our knowledge is built would be based on a delusion. There is, I should add, the possibility that there is still an accidental correspondence between our idealistic mental machinery and reality haphazardly established by evolution. Like hunters that throw spears at ripples in the water having no idea there are fish or that there are fish beneath the ripples. It just tends to work, and an ample supply of dead fish happen to show up at the shore for them to eat. Let’s hope this isn’t really the case. Let’s hope there is some significant correspondence between our thoughts and reality. My emotions and my body do seem to suggest so, that there is some real physical reality I am interreacting with. And because emotions and the body, as I take it, are not part of Mind, there is reason for hope.
